00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.280
So we're going to start
with a couple of examples.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.170
So I'm going to
switch over to RStudio

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:30.030
and actually want to show
you a Shiny application.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.460
And this is an application I
inherited from that prior life.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.520
Basically what it
allows someone to do

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.960
is compare the fuel economy of
two different vehicles going

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.170
from point A to point B.
And so maybe as an example,

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:51.320
we could look at a Ford Explorer
versus a Toyota Highlander

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:52.240
Hybrid.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.330
A simulation occurs
behind the scenes here.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:59.090
And then we did the
result. In this case,

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:03.680
the Ford Explorer uses
more gas than the hybrid.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:06.290
So this is an
interesting application

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:10.100
because all of that simulation
is occurring in Python.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:15.290
In fact, the code that I
inherited from my engineers

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:21.260
is this really huge,
1,000 of lines of Python

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:24.740
code that handles
the simulations here.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:28.070
And what we struggled with
before the reticulate package

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:31.300
was how to make this code
accessible to others.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.700
No one on our team had
the skill to build out

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:38.280
a full-fledged web application
framework in Python.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.390
And so what we would end
up doing is taking requests

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:44.150
from different stakeholders,
we would run these functions

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.970
manually, and then we
would return the results.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.250
And it was a really
time-intensive and laborous

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:50.670
process.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.820
So when the reticulate
package came out,

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:55.340
I was excited to
be able to build

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:59.350
using the amazing tools
available in R around Shiny.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:02.570
I was excited to build this
type of application that

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:05.960
would allow those stakeholders
to run experiments

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:08.077
interactively themselves.

00:08:08.077 --> 00:08:10.160
And so I want to show you
a little bit of the code

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.770
just so you get a feel
for what this looks like.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:17.000
Inside of the application, we're
using the reticulate package

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:20.080
to source this Python file.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.840
And this Python file has a whole
bunch of functions inside of it

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:25.530
that do all the heavy lifting.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:29.390
You can see one of them
here is a sim drive.

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:33.650
And once you've sourced that
file, all you have to do

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:37.909
is call it, call that function
as if it were an R function.

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:40.370
You can see that call here.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:42.380
And the reticulate
package takes care

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.330
of all the dirty work for you.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:48.350
So you don't have to worry about
managing a subprocess yourself.

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:50.460
You don't have to deal
with type conversions.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:52.520
Our data frames
on the R side are

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:54.200
going to become
those dictionaries

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:55.760
that the Python function needs.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:57.410
And then the Python
function is going

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.440
to return results that
are nicely handled back

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.320
into a tidy data frame.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.510
And so a lot of that heavy
lifting is done for you,

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:07.640
and it becomes really
seamless to get

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:13.110
started calling that Python
code from the R interface.

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.740
Now when you're doing
that type of work,

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:19.100
combining R and Python
together, it's not only

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.010
enough to be able to
do that seamlessly,

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:25.490
but you also typically don't
want to have to context switch.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:28.280
And so that leads to the second
thing I want to show you,

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:31.100
which is that inside
of RStudio, again,

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:34.040
based on the open
source work that we've

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:35.990
done with the
reticulate package,

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.390
you're able to interact
with Python files directly.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.440
And so this is a pretty
simple Python file.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:45.110
And what I can do
inside of RStudio

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:48.140
is execute the
code line by line.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:53.000
In this case, we're using
NumPy and MatPlotLib to create

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.590
a bivariate distribution plot.

00:09:55.590 --> 00:10:00.020
You can see the MatPlotLib image
shows up in the plots panel.

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:02.270
Our Python code is
being executed line

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:03.890
by line in the console.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:06.860
And we even get some of
the really nice ergonomics

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:10.010
that you would expect from
an IDE, such as autocomplete

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:13.470
in the Python context,
as well as help.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.350
And this is really
exciting because if you

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:18.230
are inheriting
those Python files

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:20.540
or gluing them
together with R code,

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:22.550
it can be really nice
to be able to interact

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:24.590
with the Python
files in one place

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:28.080
without having to switch context
to a totally different editor

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.700
or a different tool.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:35.670
So RStudio by no means is
going to be a full-fledged IDE

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:37.910
for Python development
at this time,

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:40.140
but in my experience
as a data scientist

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.490
working with both of the
languages it meets my needs

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:47.820
and has a lot of the
things I'm looking for.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:50.540
So that's how you might interact
with a Python file directly.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.320
There's one other integration
that we've built into the IDE

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.240
to make working with
R and Python together

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:59.140
pretty seamless, and
that's through R Markdown.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.630
R Markdown, if you're
not familiar with it

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:04.300
and you're coming from the
Python side of the world,

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:08.960
it's very similar in its
goal to a Jupyter Notebook.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.700
It allows you to do what we
call literate programming, where

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:18.070
you're going to combine text
and prose alongside the output

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:21.640
of code and all in a
single scientific notebook

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.830
or computational document.

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:25.810
That's what R Markdown
allows you to do,

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.930
but it's a little bit of
a misnomer, R Markdown,

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:30.820
because you can actually
use a whole bunch

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:34.150
of different languages inside
of an R Markdown document.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:35.650
Of course, as you
might guess, we're

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:36.910
talking about Python today.

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:38.410
So what I'm going
to show you is how

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:41.770
you can use Python in R
inside of this document.

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:44.560
We'll start by inserting
a Python code chunk.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:49.360
And then let's load some
data from the Seaborn Python

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.630
library.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:55.590
And specifically, we're going to
look at our favorite Iris data

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:56.770
set.

00:11:56.770 --> 00:12:00.880
You can see the IDEs
autocomplete at work,

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.700
helping me get these
function calls right.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:08.740
And then we're going to use
some Panda syntax to subset

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.780
this data set that we've loaded.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:18.220
And specifically, we're going
to look at one species of Iris

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:20.382
called setosa.

00:12:20.382 --> 00:12:25.285
And actually, let's not overload
our variable names here.

00:12:25.285 --> 00:12:27.550
We'll call this setosa.

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:31.570
And we can execute this
right inside of RStudio

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.490
in the Python context.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:36.740
We can double check that
we've got things right here.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:38.830
And so this does look
like the Iris data

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:42.230
set with the setosa species.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:44.410
So we're looking good so far.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.000
What I want to
show you and where

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.540
things get magical
and really exciting

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:52.150
is that from our
R code chunk, we

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:56.080
can now access everything we
were just doing in the Python

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:57.430
environment.

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:03.430
So specifically, let's
load the ggplot2 library.

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:10.630
And we're going to take a look
at plotting this data set.

00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:12.640
And the way this
works is there's

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.130
a special object in
the R environment

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:19.470
called pi that gives us access
to the Python environment

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:22.760
and all the variables or data
that is in that environment.

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:27.010
So here, we have pi$setosa
that's going to give us access

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:30.280
to that Pandas data frame.

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:33.350
And we can go ahead and plot it.

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:34.720
We don't have to--

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:41.330
from there, we can do
everything as if we were in R.

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:45.670
We'll just make a
simple plot here.

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:48.400
And what's really
interesting about this is it

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.920
allows us to use the
languages for what

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:52.030
they're really good at.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:54.100
So as an example
here, we're using

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:56.440
Pandas, which is a pretty
powerful tool for data

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:59.590
manipulation, and then
ggplot2, which is really

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:01.775
excellent for visualization.

00:14:01.775 --> 00:14:05.190
And so we can run this r code.

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:09.010
And, of course, we
get an error here.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:10.840
Let's maybe see if
we can figure out

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:13.270
what's going on with
this error real quick.

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:14.770
One of the things
that's really nice

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:18.280
is with that Python
environment, I

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:20.940
can actually use some
of the IDE's features

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:22.970
to explore what's going on.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:25.210
So as an example, I
can view the data set

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:28.690
that's in Python using
the view command.

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:31.360
And it looks like this
is the data set we saw.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:34.963
It's the Iris filter down
to the setosa species.

00:14:34.963 --> 00:14:36.880
And actually, I think I
know what I did wrong.

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:39.490
It looks like on the Python
version of this data set,

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:41.745
we have underscores.

00:14:41.745 --> 00:14:43.370
I used periods.

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:47.720
So if we replace those
periods with underscores--

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:51.190
there we go-- we get our nice
ggplot2 of the relationship

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:54.590
between length and width.

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:56.690
So those are just
a couple of ways

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:58.520
that a data
scientist can combine

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:00.710
R and Python inside of RStudio.

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:03.320
All of that is using
the open source

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:05.570
tools that's going to
be available wherever

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:07.040
you're running RStudio.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.140
At the heart of it is
that reticulate package.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:14.690
The reticulate package uses
the Python and R C++ APIs.

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:16.880
So the data conversions
are pretty fast.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:18.770
It does use a little
bit more memory than

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:20.660
if you were using
a single language,

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:22.340
but it allows you
to take advantage

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:24.960
of tools in each language.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:27.070
So you can use R for
what it's really good at

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:29.330
and Python for what
it's really good at.

00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:32.100
And just to recap
what we saw there,

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:34.990
there is a combination of R,
and Python, and R Markdown

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:36.480
that we just looked at.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.150
We also saw at
the very beginning

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:41.750
how you can use Python functions
and execute Python code

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:45.610
in the context of R
scripts or functions,

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:48.380
which is really useful if you
want to build a Shiny app that

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:50.400
pulls in Python code.

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:53.510
And then you can also just
edit and play with Python code

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:56.440
directly in RStudio as the IDE.

00:15:56.440 --> 00:16:00.480
Now that's really useful
for a data scientist

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:03.010
to glue these two
languages together.

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:05.140
What we see is
that often it's not

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:08.640
just a data scientist who has
to worry about R and Python,

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:10.280
and the combination of the two.

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:12.310
Especially in
organizations, there's

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:14.770
another really
important team that

00:16:14.770 --> 00:16:16.750
gets involved with
these two languages,

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:20.200
and that's the DevOps
or IT administration

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:24.220
group that is responsible for
creating that environment where

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:27.160
data scientists can be
productive, and have access

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:31.390
to data, and collaborate
with one another.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:33.850
What we tend to see,
working with a lot

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:36.910
of these organizations
as they're adopting R,

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:42.040
is that it can be a challenge
to create an infrastructure that

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:45.910
meets the needs of all
these different languages.

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:48.580
And what happens-- you're
in the worst case scenario--

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:50.650
is that data
scientists will just

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.410
continue doing work
on their own desktops

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:54.840
where they're most comfortable.

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:57.740
And that becomes really
hard to troubleshoot.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:00.880
We've talked to IT admins
who have hundreds of tickets

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:04.780
in their system along the lines
of this version of a Python

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:07.660
package isn't installing,
and so I can't share

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.880
a notebook with my colleague.

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:13.450
And so what we're excited
to show you is RStudio's

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:15.250
professional products--

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:18.819
RStudio Server and RStudio
Connect, which together

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:20.920
make up RStudio Team.

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:25.270
And these professional products
allow the DevOps and IT group

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:28.000
to provide that
single infrastructure

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.340
where data science teams can
use R and Python together.

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:33.470
And they can do it in
their favorite tools,

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:37.240
whether that's RStudio or
Jupyter, without resorting back

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:38.900
to their desktop.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:42.670
That makes it easier for them to
collaborate and work together,

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:47.350
but it also means that IT only
has to configure, maintain,

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:51.400
scale a single environment
instead of dealing

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:53.770
with different tools
for different languages

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.240
across different teams.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:58.630
And so I want to show you what
that looks like by actually

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:00.670
going back to RStudio.

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:04.030
And one of the things that those
of you with really astute eyes

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:06.010
might have noticed
is that I'm actually

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:09.232
using RStudio inside
of a web browser, which

00:18:09.232 --> 00:18:11.440
might be a little bit
different, especially if you're

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:14.605
used to using RStudio as
a desktop application.

00:18:14.605 --> 00:18:17.890
And the reason that you see
RStudio presented in a web

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:20.980
browser is because all
the computation here

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:22.810
is happening on a server.

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:26.380
And what that means is that
Lou, myself, and the other data

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.810
scientists at RStudio
are all working

00:18:28.810 --> 00:18:30.520
on a consistent platform.

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:33.530
So it's easier for us to
share work with one another.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:36.220
It's easier for us to get
access to the production data

00:18:36.220 --> 00:18:38.050
that we use at RStudio.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:42.730
And one of my favorite parts
is that I have a single entry

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:45.670
point for all the
different types of projects

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:46.330
that I work on.

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:49.570
And so this is the
RStudio Server home page.

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:52.000
You can see the Shiny
application, that

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:56.500
project we were just in, is
sitting idle now on the server.

00:18:56.500 --> 00:19:00.020
I have a job that I kicked
off a couple of hours ago

00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:01.570
that's running in
the background.

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:04.540
I don't have to worry about
desktop updates interfering

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:06.260
with that job.

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:09.790
And the new thing that
we wanted to showcase

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:13.270
is that when you go to start
a new session on RStudio,

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:15.970
you now get to select
what editor you want.

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:19.450
So, of course, you can
choose the RStudio IDE,

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:21.820
and RStudio Server
will run that for you.

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:24.910
But you can also pick between
things like JupyterLab

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:26.900
and the Jupyter Notebook.

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:28.780
I'll select Jupyter Notebook.

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:30.800
And we'll kick this
off in a second.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:32.710
But before we do
that, I also want

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:35.530
to call out another choice
that's really powerful,

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:39.140
which is where you want the
computation to actually run.

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:43.530
So traditionally you would
normally run R and Python

00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:46.720
on the same environment,
in the same location where

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:49.890
RStudio Server was running.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:53.380
In one of our goals to make the
server and this infrastructure

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:55.630
more extensible
and easier for IT,

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:58.300
we've added the
ability to offload

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:00.700
some of that computation
to other locations.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:03.580
And so specifically what
you're looking at here

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:07.120
is an integration between
RStudio Server and a Kubernetes

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:08.800
cluster.

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:11.950
Our Kubernetes cluster
happens to be running in AWS,

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:14.470
but you can run it if
you're running Kubernetes

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:18.220
on any of the cloud providers
or even on premise yourself.

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:20.530
And what this allows me to
do as a data scientist is

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:23.450
without learning any new
tools or new workbenches,

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:25.930
right from within
RStudio Server I

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:30.020
can pick the profile of tasks
that I'm going to have--

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:33.940
so what CPUs I need, the
size of memory that I want.

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:36.970
And then my IT group
has put together

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:39.610
a couple of images,
Docker images that'll

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.820
control the environment
where this Jupyter Notebook

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:44.810
is going to run.

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:46.390
So this gives a
lot of flexibility

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:49.690
for our Team to specify exactly
what we're looking for while

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:51.350
still scaling elastically.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:53.260
And that's all
inside of RStudio.

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:55.630
We don't have to jump
between different tools

00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:57.600
or different environments.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.240
And so if I start
this session, you'll

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:02.410
see on the home page
the RStudio IDE,

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:06.070
as well as the Jupyter Notebook.

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:08.970
I can fire up this
Jupyter Notebook,

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:12.750
and it's going to give me the
same files, the same access

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:14.820
that I had from RStudio.

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:17.170
And so like many
data scientists,

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:19.590
I don't do a great job
cleaning up my workspace.

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:22.080
I have a whole bunch of
things going on here.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:26.250
But I can open up a
specific set of examples

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:28.230
that I created for today.

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:30.750
All these examples in code
are going to be online,

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:33.045
and we're happy to share
them after the webinar.

00:21:33.045 --> 00:21:35.670
But I just want to show you what
it looks like to run a Jupyter

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:37.170
Notebook here.

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:41.100
And it actually might be
a little bit of a letdown

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:44.610
because what it looks like
is a Jupyter Notebook.

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:49.140
You're able to execute the
code chunks inside of Jupyter.

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:50.700
You can see the results.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:52.800
And we've tried
really hard at RStudio

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:56.380
to give you as short a path
as possible to the tool

00:21:56.380 --> 00:21:58.260
that you're comfortable with.

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:00.840
So we don't require a
steep learning curve

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:02.250
around anything else.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:05.940
We just give you access
to Jupyter right away.

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:09.270
Inside of this notebook
though, we are still in that

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:12.770
server-side environment, which
is really nice because it means

00:22:12.770 --> 00:22:17.730
that the Python kernels
that we have are shared--

00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:20.500
can be shared amongst
different data scientists.

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:23.190
So if you're onboarding
someone new to Python,

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.530
they're going to have access
to that shared environment

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:27.990
that your IT group has
set up, in this case that

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:30.360
shared Docker image,
so that we're all

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:32.590
playing from the same playbook.

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:35.310
And while that's desirable
a lot of the time,

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:38.190
for advanced Python
power users you still

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:40.440
have the ability to
create your own kernels,

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:42.420
set up your own virtual
or common environments

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:43.900
for specific projects.

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:47.860
So all of that same tooling
is available to you.

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:50.140
The only difference between
this Jupyter Notebook

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:53.920
launch through RStudio
Server and a Jupyter Notebook

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:57.800
you might run yourself are two
buttons that you'll see here.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:01.140
So one is this RStudio icon.

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:03.340
And you can tell it
really is a love story.

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:06.360
The icons are even sitting
next to each other.

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:09.787
And this just takes you back,
if you want, to your home page,

00:23:09.787 --> 00:23:11.620
where you can see all
the different projects

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:14.710
and contexts that
you're working on.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:16.810
Inside the Jupyter
Notebook, if we go back

00:23:16.810 --> 00:23:21.130
to that running notebook, the
other icon that you'll notice

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:22.690
is this Publish button.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:24.920
And we'll talk about
that in just a second.

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:28.750
So remember that icon.

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:32.040
So to summarize
for DevOps and IT,

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:34.080
one of the things that's
really critical when

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.180
you have R and
Python users is being

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:39.300
able to provide them a
consistent entry point that

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:42.450
has their favorite tools without
introducing a whole bunch

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:44.920
of headaches and hurdles.

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:46.890
And RStudio Team is
an easy way to do

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:51.990
that in a single product only
configured and integrated once.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:56.220
You're able to give folks access
to the RStudio IDE on the R

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.620
side, which folks
know and love, as well

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:00.570
as their favorite tools
on the Python side,

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:02.560
such as Jupyter Notebooks.

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:06.150
And we'll be extending that
suite of editors as well.

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:08.850
And that corresponds
to an extension

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:10.260
in the number of back ends too.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:12.520
So I showed you
Kubernetes as one way

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:14.640
to run and execute this content.

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:17.730
There's also
plug-ins if you want

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:20.580
to run these different editors
on more traditional HPC

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:24.875
infrastructure that you might
have at your organization.

00:24:24.875 --> 00:24:26.250
So we've covered
data scientists.

00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:28.410
We've covered IT and ops.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:32.370
There's a third set
of important folks

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:34.170
that Lou mentioned
at the beginning who

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:35.880
care about R and Python.

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:39.390
And that's the data science
team leaders or data science

00:24:39.390 --> 00:24:42.960
managers and ultimately
the business stakeholders.

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:45.990
And we combine these
together on a single slide

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:50.550
because we believe their
concerns are largely

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:53.280
two sides of the same coin.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:55.360
On the one hand,
data science leaders,

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:57.810
they're looking at,
how do I onboard

00:24:57.810 --> 00:25:01.290
new users into these different
ecosystems where they can

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:03.720
collaborate with the
team and without having

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:07.530
to reinvent wheels or spend
time doing translation?

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:11.880
I was talking to one manager
at a pharmaceutical company.

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:14.790
They're trying to get
more than 500 SAS users

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:16.590
into these open source tools.

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:19.560
So it becomes really
important that as you

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:21.420
do those trainings
and onboardings,

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:24.620
it's a seamless process.

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:27.170
Business stakeholders
meanwhile, they

00:25:27.170 --> 00:25:29.540
share some of the same goals
with a data science leader.

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:32.390
They really want to see a return
on their investment in the data

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:33.980
science team.

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:36.530
And ultimately what
they care about more so

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:40.220
than whether you use R on
Python is just getting insights

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:42.890
from the team so that they
can make good decisions

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:46.010
and not rely only
on their intuition.

00:25:46.010 --> 00:25:48.470
And critical to doing
that is their ability

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:50.720
to interact with
the team quickly.

00:25:50.720 --> 00:25:54.860
We want to avoid slow iteration
times and emails back and forth

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:59.774
asking for requests between
the different team members.

00:25:59.774 --> 00:26:02.160
And so the last
thing I'll show you

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:06.930
is how RStudio Team in
combination with R and Python

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:09.768
allows you to address
some of these challenges.

00:26:09.768 --> 00:26:11.310
And specifically,
we're going to look

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:14.400
at what it means to take some
of those Python and R data

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:18.300
products and put them
into production on a tool

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:21.620
that we call RStudio Connect.

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:25.820
And so I'm going to switch back
here to the Shiny application

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:28.840
that we started the demo with.

00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:33.560
And this application, as we
mentioned at the beginning,

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:37.270
one of the things that's really
nice about a Shiny application

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:41.050
is that it gives you the
ability to allow stakeholders

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:43.780
who may have specific
domain expertise

00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:46.930
to play around with your
code and run experiments.

00:26:46.930 --> 00:26:51.070
The challenge here is that right
now this application is trapped

00:26:51.070 --> 00:26:53.050
on my development environment.

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:54.360
It's not easy to share.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:27:00.080
And if I close out of RStudio,
the application goes away.

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:03.040
And so what we need to do
is move it into production,

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:04.870
into a place where
others are going

00:27:04.870 --> 00:27:07.330
to be able to interact
with it and rely on it.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:11.410
And in RStudio, it's easy to do
that through that Publish icon

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:13.100
we were talking about.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:16.630
So all I have to do to deploy
this to my production connect

00:27:16.630 --> 00:27:20.050
server, which is a server
that's running on premise--

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:22.930
I'm not locked into a
platform as a service.

00:27:22.930 --> 00:27:26.140
I'm not sending my data
outside my firewall.

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:30.120
I'm just deploying to another
server with a production

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:30.850
intent--

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:32.190
is click Publish.

00:27:32.190 --> 00:27:34.660
And what's going to happen
is that the R and Python

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:36.910
environments are going
to be identified.

00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:40.370
All the different files and
packages that are in use

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:44.133
are going to be listed and then
sent to RStudio Connect, where

00:27:44.133 --> 00:27:45.800
that environment is
going to be restored

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:50.080
and the application
is going to be run.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:54.490
Kind of like those old cooking
shows, this is the ingredients,

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:56.930
and I'm going to
jump to the end,

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:59.320
pull the final product
out of the oven.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:02.320
But this is where the
application will live

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:04.210
and what it will
look like after it's

00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:07.090
deployed to RStudio Connect.

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:09.910
And so the application
here will load for us.

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:11.620
It's that same
application we've been

00:28:11.620 --> 00:28:13.840
playing with the whole
time, but now it's

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:15.650
in a production context.

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:17.120
And so what does that mean?

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:20.650
Well, very critically it
means that I have a stable URL

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:22.480
I can share with stakeholders.

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:25.270
And then they can go and
access this dashboard

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:27.100
and not even need
to know that it

00:28:27.100 --> 00:28:28.660
was written in R or in Python.

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:30.160
They don't need to
know the details.

00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:32.590
It just becomes a
web application.

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:34.680
I also have a lot
of those things

00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:36.380
that IT is going to care about.

00:28:36.380 --> 00:28:40.120
So I can specify specific
users so that this environment

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:44.260
is secure and locked down
to specific users or groups.

00:28:44.260 --> 00:28:46.090
I can look at the
logs so I don't

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:49.960
have to email someone
in IT to try to download

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:51.600
a log file and send it to me.

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:56.050
I can actually look at those and
iterate and debug in real time.

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:58.580
Then also very
critically, I can scale.

00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:01.600
And so we used RStudio
Connect to scale

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:05.000
to more than 10,000
users at a single time,

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:09.130
so 10,000 concurrent users
looking at a dashboard that

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:10.720
involves both R and Python.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:13.330
So it's really good at
managing all of those processes

00:29:13.330 --> 00:29:15.606
and connections for you.

00:29:15.606 --> 00:29:18.760
So that's an example
of a Shiny application

00:29:18.760 --> 00:29:22.660
that is going to allow business
stakeholders to get value

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:24.880
really quickly and to be
able to iterate and run

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:28.030
their own experiments, but
dashboards and applications

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:31.510
aren't the only thing that
data science teams create.

00:29:31.510 --> 00:29:33.520
In fact, one of
the things that we

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.290
were talking about
at the beginning

00:29:35.290 --> 00:29:37.030
was those Jupyter Notebooks.

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:40.870
And so you can publish Jupyter
Notebooks to RStudio Connect,

00:29:40.870 --> 00:29:43.630
and that becomes a really
powerful way for teams

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:44.710
to share their work.

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:47.170
You can have a
notebook that documents

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:48.950
your experiment, your thinking.

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:51.440
And that'll be
available to everyone.

00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:53.860
And we have that support
for Jupyter Notebooks,

00:29:53.860 --> 00:29:55.390
but also for R Markdown.

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:57.940
So it doesn't matter what
language you're using,

00:29:57.940 --> 00:30:00.580
your work can be shared
in a single place that

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:03.610
becomes a ground truth for
the knowledge the data science

00:30:03.610 --> 00:30:05.015
team has.

00:30:05.015 --> 00:30:07.390
The other thing that you can
do with these notebooks that

00:30:07.390 --> 00:30:10.835
gets really powerful is once
they're deployed to Connect,

00:30:10.835 --> 00:30:13.450
Connect can run
them on a schedule.

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:17.000
And so I actually want to show
you an example of an R Markdown

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:18.090
document.

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:21.500
This document is scheduled
to run twice a week.

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:24.920
We can look at the historical
versions of this document that

00:30:24.920 --> 00:30:26.530
have been published over time.

00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:29.030
But one of the things that's
really interesting about this R

00:30:29.030 --> 00:30:32.090
Markdown document is that
it's designed to communicate

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:33.380
with stakeholders.

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:36.620
So essentially what
it's doing is twice

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:38.090
a week on Mondays
and Wednesdays,

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:40.370
it checks inventory supplies.

00:30:40.370 --> 00:30:43.490
It runs a forecast and
a burndown analysis.

00:30:43.490 --> 00:30:45.050
And then if there's
a discrepancy

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:47.660
between the inventory
and the forecast,

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:51.920
it actually uses code in
RStudio Connect's scheduler

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:55.730
to send an email to
stakeholders, in this case

00:30:55.730 --> 00:30:57.150
the supply team.

00:30:57.150 --> 00:31:00.470
And that email is fully
customized through the code.

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:02.030
So you can include results.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:04.130
You can include plots.

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:06.740
And those are going to show
up in your stakeholder's inbox

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:08.750
so they can look at it
right on their phone.

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.460
And that's helpful
in those cases

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:14.510
where people want to get
alerts or monitor something,

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:17.645
not having to constantly
refer back to a dashboard.

00:31:17.645 --> 00:31:20.450
And that's available
for R Markdown documents

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:23.860
that are, again, allowing you to
combine Python and R together.

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:25.730
So it's a powerful
way to operationalize

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:29.220
some of that work that
the team is doing.

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:32.470
But one other piece of
content or pillar of content

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:34.440
that I want to
show you on Connect

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:37.930
is how you might go
about deploying a model.

00:31:37.930 --> 00:31:41.350
So often, data science teams
are responsible for creating

00:31:41.350 --> 00:31:42.490
models.

00:31:42.490 --> 00:31:45.820
And we've seen how models
could be shared with people

00:31:45.820 --> 00:31:49.810
in something like a
dashboard or a notebook,

00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:51.940
but often you also need
to share those models

00:31:51.940 --> 00:31:53.350
with other services.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:54.670
Maybe it's a website.

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:56.460
Maybe it's an application.

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:58.420
Maybe it's something like Java.

00:31:58.420 --> 00:32:02.050
But those tools want to consume
the smart insights that you've

00:32:02.050 --> 00:32:03.820
created in R and Python.

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:05.800
And the way that you
can do that easily

00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:09.167
is by hosting your
model as a RESTful API.

00:32:09.167 --> 00:32:11.500
So I just want to give you
an example of what that looks

00:32:11.500 --> 00:32:14.742
like on RStudio Connect,
the code for this API

00:32:14.742 --> 00:32:17.200
so you get a sense for what it
looks like to actually write

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:17.700
it.

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:20.480
It's something we'll
share after the webinar.

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:24.470
But in this case, our model
is a sentiment predictor.

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:28.390
So essentially the
model takes as an input

00:32:28.390 --> 00:32:32.260
a string, so maybe something
like the word "debugging."

00:32:32.260 --> 00:32:35.690
And we call this
model on Connect

00:32:35.690 --> 00:32:37.520
using a RESTful request.

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:38.793
It looks like this.

00:32:38.793 --> 00:32:40.460
So this is something
a software engineer

00:32:40.460 --> 00:32:42.320
would be really familiar with.

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:45.980
And then the model
returns the sentiment,

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:50.570
0 being negative if the
phrase was not very happy

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:52.430
or 1 being really positive.

00:32:52.430 --> 00:32:56.660
In this case, debugging
is not very positive.

00:32:56.660 --> 00:33:02.420
If we send the model
something like "great beer,"

00:33:02.420 --> 00:33:06.960
turns out that great beer
is quite a bit more positive

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:08.930
and much closer to 1.

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:13.460
And so this model is using
R. It's also using Python.

00:33:13.460 --> 00:33:16.220
A Python library called
spaCy is actually what's

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:18.330
giving us the sentiment scores.

00:33:18.330 --> 00:33:22.190
And just like we saw with the
application, the Shiny app,

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:25.850
RStudio Connect is handling
the access controls, the logs,

00:33:25.850 --> 00:33:29.180
scaling the number of
processes to handle requests.

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:32.220
So there's a lot
of flexibility here

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:33.815
to operationalize these models.

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:39.890
And so to summarize,
really what we're after

00:33:39.890 --> 00:33:43.340
is making it easier for people
to make important decisions

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:44.750
with data.

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:47.150
And that's going
to require teams

00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:49.790
to use R and Python together.

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:51.350
And there are some
considerations

00:33:51.350 --> 00:33:53.720
for team leaders
and stakeholders

00:33:53.720 --> 00:33:55.400
when they set out to do that.

00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:57.740
The big ones are being able
to access those insights

00:33:57.740 --> 00:33:59.870
consistently and
reliably, which is really

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:01.470
what production means.

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:04.430
And for teams, it's being
able to deliver that content

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:07.110
regularly and seamlessly.

00:34:07.110 --> 00:34:10.130
And so that's what RStudio
Team and specifically

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:12.335
RStudio Connect
is designed to do,

00:34:12.335 --> 00:34:17.150
is to help those decision
makers really rely on those data

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:20.300
insights and to ultimately
get a return on the investment

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:24.100
from the data scientists'
work in any language.

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:27.260
And so with that, I'm going to
hand things back over to Lou

00:34:27.260 --> 00:34:30.020
to recap what we've talked
about and look ahead

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:33.205
into the future a little bit.

00:34:33.205 --> 00:34:34.330
LOU BAJUK: Thank you, Sean.

00:34:34.330 --> 00:34:35.115
I appreciate that.

