00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:18.580
So Sean, we've got a
number of questions here.

00:43:18.580 --> 00:43:23.218
One that comes up a
few times is, can you

00:43:23.218 --> 00:43:24.760
clarify the difference
between what's

00:43:24.760 --> 00:43:27.640
available in our open
source products and the work

00:43:27.640 --> 00:43:29.622
we provide there,
and what's unique

00:43:29.622 --> 00:43:31.330
to our commercial
products around support

00:43:31.330 --> 00:43:33.800
for Python and Jupyter?

00:43:33.800 --> 00:43:35.180
SEAN LOPP: Yeah, absolutely.

00:43:35.180 --> 00:43:39.050
So we're dedicated to
making open source tools

00:43:39.050 --> 00:43:41.300
available for data scientists.

00:43:41.300 --> 00:43:42.920
And so everything
that we were looking

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:46.160
at at the very
beginning is open source

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:47.750
through reticulate package.

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:50.300
And that includes everything
that you saw inside

00:43:50.300 --> 00:43:51.800
of the RStudio IDE--

00:43:51.800 --> 00:43:54.800
so the ability to switch back
and forth between languages

00:43:54.800 --> 00:43:57.050
inside of R
Markdown, the ability

00:43:57.050 --> 00:44:00.650
to run Python scripts
inside of the IDE,

00:44:00.650 --> 00:44:04.220
as well as the reticulate
package's capabilities

00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:07.880
for combining the R and
Python runtimes, for example,

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:11.690
the Shiny application that
was calling Python functions.

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:14.390
That's all available in
the open source ecosystem,

00:44:14.390 --> 00:44:16.910
and you can get started
today on your desktop

00:44:16.910 --> 00:44:19.213
with the reticulate package.

00:44:19.213 --> 00:44:20.630
The commercial
products are really

00:44:20.630 --> 00:44:23.720
geared for those organizations
who are supporting teams

00:44:23.720 --> 00:44:25.740
working in both languages.

00:44:25.740 --> 00:44:29.180
And so as we were talking about
IT, as well as the business

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:31.910
stakeholders, we
showed some examples

00:44:31.910 --> 00:44:34.910
of RStudio's professional
team products.

00:44:34.910 --> 00:44:36.890
And that's specifically
going to be

00:44:36.890 --> 00:44:40.580
when you saw RStudio Server
working with Kubernetes to run

00:44:40.580 --> 00:44:41.750
a Jupyter Notebook.

00:44:41.750 --> 00:44:44.300
That's limited to the
commercial products.

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:48.350
And then RStudio Connect, which
is that platform for sharing

00:44:48.350 --> 00:44:50.120
production assets,
whether they're

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:52.790
applications, or notebooks,
or reports, that's

00:44:52.790 --> 00:44:54.865
a commercial product as well.

00:44:54.865 --> 00:44:55.740
LOU BAJUK: Excellent.

00:44:55.740 --> 00:44:57.350
Thank you, Sean.

00:44:57.350 --> 00:45:01.040
So I'm going to try and
synthesize several questions

00:45:01.040 --> 00:45:03.970
here because there are
a couple of big groups

00:45:03.970 --> 00:45:07.220
that they fall into, some
of them fall into at least.

00:45:07.220 --> 00:45:09.800
One is-- there's a
series of questions

00:45:09.800 --> 00:45:13.850
that's along the lines of,
why should I learn Python?

00:45:13.850 --> 00:45:15.920
One questioner asked,
does the data scientist

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:18.290
need to know all the languages?

00:45:18.290 --> 00:45:20.480
Another questioner asks,
what's better in R?

00:45:20.480 --> 00:45:24.200
Another questioner asks,
what's better in Python?

00:45:24.200 --> 00:45:28.760
And one questioner suggests
that in R, ggplot and Shiny

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:32.120
are particular strengths of
R that Python users might

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:33.120
want to leverage.

00:45:33.120 --> 00:45:34.793
So any thoughts there?

00:45:34.793 --> 00:45:35.460
SEAN LOPP: Yeah.

00:45:35.460 --> 00:45:37.800
Those are great questions.

00:45:37.800 --> 00:45:40.820
I think what we've
really come to appreciate

00:45:40.820 --> 00:45:45.770
is that pitting R and
Python against one another

00:45:45.770 --> 00:45:48.680
just isn't really practical
for data scientists and data

00:45:48.680 --> 00:45:50.120
science teams.

00:45:50.120 --> 00:45:52.250
At the end of the
day, people are coming

00:45:52.250 --> 00:45:53.673
from different backgrounds.

00:45:53.673 --> 00:45:55.590
Some have learned R.
Some have learned Python.

00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:57.400
Some have learned both.

00:45:57.400 --> 00:46:00.890
And so really the
conversation to have

00:46:00.890 --> 00:46:04.130
is, how do you use R
and Python together,

00:46:04.130 --> 00:46:09.620
that love story idea, as opposed
to, which language is best?

00:46:09.620 --> 00:46:12.830
Both are going to be
around for the long term.

00:46:12.830 --> 00:46:16.780
And it is really going to
depend on your context.

00:46:16.780 --> 00:46:21.330
There's a lot of domain-specific
packages in both languages.

00:46:21.330 --> 00:46:24.380
So if you're working in
the insurance industry

00:46:24.380 --> 00:46:25.940
versus in the
finance industry, you

00:46:25.940 --> 00:46:28.910
might find yourself
using different packages

00:46:28.910 --> 00:46:31.280
for different needs.

00:46:31.280 --> 00:46:33.740
So I know that's a little
bit of dodging the question.

00:46:33.740 --> 00:46:37.850
The key thing, I think,
is to not get hung up on,

00:46:37.850 --> 00:46:40.995
should I only learn R, or
should I only learn Python?

00:46:40.995 --> 00:46:43.370
Really, both languages are
going to be needed to succeed.

00:46:45.995 --> 00:46:46.870
LOU BAJUK: Excellent.

00:46:46.870 --> 00:46:49.280
Thank you.

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:50.960
There's another
series of questions

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:54.630
that are all around
configuration and versioning.

00:46:54.630 --> 00:46:57.890
There is one set of
questions that asks, well,

00:46:57.890 --> 00:46:59.000
I don't see this option.

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:01.880
What version of RStudio
products do I need?

00:47:01.880 --> 00:47:04.190
There is another
question that asks,

00:47:04.190 --> 00:47:08.010
does this support
Python 2 or 3, or both?

00:47:08.010 --> 00:47:09.592
And then third, a
more general set

00:47:09.592 --> 00:47:11.300
of questions around,
how is installation,

00:47:11.300 --> 00:47:13.040
and configuration,
and versioning

00:47:13.040 --> 00:47:15.063
of Pythoning handled?

00:47:15.063 --> 00:47:15.730
SEAN LOPP: Yeah.

00:47:15.730 --> 00:47:18.010
Those are really
great questions.

00:47:18.010 --> 00:47:23.530
So let me quickly go
back to RStudio IDE.

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:26.710
RStudio 1.2 is going
to be the version that

00:47:26.710 --> 00:47:29.980
has this built-in support.

00:47:29.980 --> 00:47:32.100
So that's what you
should look for.

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:33.820
And you can see your
version of RStudio

00:47:33.820 --> 00:47:36.070
in just Help, and about RStudio.

00:47:36.070 --> 00:47:38.810
1.2 is what you're
going to look for there.

00:47:38.810 --> 00:47:41.650
You'll also want to install
the reticulate package.

00:47:41.650 --> 00:47:44.860
That's going to unlock a
lot of this capability.

00:47:44.860 --> 00:47:47.950
And if you do try to use an
R Markdown document with R

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:50.110
and Python, the
IDE can guide you

00:47:50.110 --> 00:47:54.260
towards installing
that package as well.

00:47:54.260 --> 00:47:57.290
So that's the jumping off point.

00:47:57.290 --> 00:48:01.330
There's two topics
around configuration.

00:48:01.330 --> 00:48:04.960
So one is, how do I
handle configuration

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:06.723
if I'm working with others?

00:48:06.723 --> 00:48:08.140
And we talked a
lot in the webinar

00:48:08.140 --> 00:48:11.320
about how it's helpful
to be running RStudio

00:48:11.320 --> 00:48:13.540
on a server-side
environment, where

00:48:13.540 --> 00:48:16.690
IT can work with you to have
a consistent environment

00:48:16.690 --> 00:48:20.290
for the team so that when you
get started either on a Jupyter

00:48:20.290 --> 00:48:24.700
Notebook or inside of
RStudio, you have a shared

00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:27.430
playbook that's a
baseline environment,

00:48:27.430 --> 00:48:31.360
whether that's R, or Python, or
the sets of packages required

00:48:31.360 --> 00:48:33.550
by both.

00:48:33.550 --> 00:48:35.650
So that would be my
recommendation, especially

00:48:35.650 --> 00:48:37.330
if you're working with others.

00:48:37.330 --> 00:48:41.830
Specific to Python
management inside of RStudio,

00:48:41.830 --> 00:48:43.990
for those of you that are
combining R and Python

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:48.400
together, the reticulate
package has a whole bunch

00:48:48.400 --> 00:48:53.270
of functions that specify what
Python environment to use.

00:48:53.270 --> 00:48:57.190
So if you're familiar with
virtual environments or conda,

00:48:57.190 --> 00:49:01.250
you can create those and
then tell R to use them.

00:49:01.250 --> 00:49:04.490
There's also functions
for creating those things.

00:49:04.490 --> 00:49:06.910
So if you're more of
an R user, but you

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:09.610
know you need to install some
Python packages to get things

00:49:09.610 --> 00:49:11.380
to work, you can
take a look at those

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:15.410
create and install functions.

00:49:15.410 --> 00:49:17.530
So there's a lot of
flexibility there.

00:49:17.530 --> 00:49:20.830
There's a reticulate cheat
sheet available online

00:49:20.830 --> 00:49:23.115
that's going to walk
through a lot of that stuff.

00:49:23.115 --> 00:49:24.490
And then the one
tip I would give

00:49:24.490 --> 00:49:29.290
folks is that the pi config--

00:49:29.290 --> 00:49:32.860
pi discover config,
so this function here,

00:49:32.860 --> 00:49:36.100
is really helpful
in that it tells you

00:49:36.100 --> 00:49:40.090
what version of Python
has been identified

00:49:40.090 --> 00:49:43.887
and is in use by the R session.

00:49:43.887 --> 00:49:45.470
Looking ahead, one
of the things we're

00:49:45.470 --> 00:49:47.840
really excited about
working on this year

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:51.297
is extending the
support for reticulate

00:49:51.297 --> 00:49:53.630
so that you're able to do a
bit more of that environment

00:49:53.630 --> 00:49:58.430
management from the RStudio
IDE with a little bit of help

00:49:58.430 --> 00:50:02.140
interactively through the UI.

00:50:02.140 --> 00:50:05.150
So hopefully that answers
some of the questions there.

00:50:05.150 --> 00:50:09.820
LOU BAJUK: There's
a question on,

00:50:09.820 --> 00:50:15.180
will this functionality be
available in RStudio Cloud?

00:50:15.180 --> 00:50:16.880
SEAN LOPP: Yeah,
excellent question.

00:50:16.880 --> 00:50:22.780
So RStudio Cloud today does not
have the Python environments

00:50:22.780 --> 00:50:24.140
installed.

00:50:24.140 --> 00:50:26.440
But if that's something
that's interesting to you,

00:50:26.440 --> 00:50:30.550
we'd love to chat about
what types of projects

00:50:30.550 --> 00:50:31.930
you're hoping to work on.

00:50:31.930 --> 00:50:34.370
For those that are
completely unfamiliar,

00:50:34.370 --> 00:50:39.670
RStudio Cloud is an
environment for--

00:50:39.670 --> 00:50:41.600
right now specifically
geared at teaching,

00:50:41.600 --> 00:50:45.520
though it also can be useful
for individual projects as well.

00:50:45.520 --> 00:50:50.540
What it essentially is is a
version of RStudio that we run.

00:50:50.540 --> 00:50:53.710
So everything we were talking
about today in the webinar

00:50:53.710 --> 00:50:55.780
was for teams that
are maintaining

00:50:55.780 --> 00:50:58.930
their own infrastructure
or running RStudio just

00:50:58.930 --> 00:51:00.850
locally on your desktop.

00:51:00.850 --> 00:51:04.540
But if you're an educator and
you want to teach students

00:51:04.540 --> 00:51:07.030
about R and Python,
it can be really

00:51:07.030 --> 00:51:10.690
annoying to have to spend the
whole first week of class just

00:51:10.690 --> 00:51:13.570
trying to get R, and RStudio,
and Python, and Jupyter

00:51:13.570 --> 00:51:14.940
all installed.

00:51:14.940 --> 00:51:18.630
The RStudio Cloud solves
that problem for you.

00:51:18.630 --> 00:51:22.210
It has support for what we
call different workspaces.

00:51:22.210 --> 00:51:25.420
You can set up a class,
invite students to that class,

00:51:25.420 --> 00:51:28.030
and then they have an
RStudio environment that's

00:51:28.030 --> 00:51:29.805
configured and ready to go.

00:51:29.805 --> 00:51:33.280
And so if you're interested in
using Python in your courses

00:51:33.280 --> 00:51:35.800
alongside of R, we'd
love to hear from you

00:51:35.800 --> 00:51:38.650
so that we can understand what
your requirements around Python

00:51:38.650 --> 00:51:39.640
might be.

00:51:39.640 --> 00:51:41.590
Someone in the
question box called us

00:51:41.590 --> 00:51:45.820
out for having Python
2.7 in our environment,

00:51:45.820 --> 00:51:47.440
and you're absolutely right.

00:51:47.440 --> 00:51:49.250
Python 2 isn't
supported anymore,

00:51:49.250 --> 00:51:51.670
but practically
it's still around.

00:51:51.670 --> 00:51:53.830
We'd love to get your
feedback, especially

00:51:53.830 --> 00:51:56.500
from educators, on what
you're hoping to teach

00:51:56.500 --> 00:51:59.020
and what that right environment
might look like for you.

00:51:59.020 --> 00:52:02.597
LOU BAJUK: We've got a question
from one of the listeners who

00:52:02.597 --> 00:52:04.180
would like to know
